===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm}} {{term|seysen|lang=enm}}, {{term|saysen|lang=enm||to seize}}, from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|seisin|lang=fro}}, {{term|saisin|lang=fro}}, from the verb {{term|seisir|lang=fro}}, {{term|saisir|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|VL.}} {{recons|sacio|saciō|lang=la}}, from the same Proto-Indo-European root as {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽|tr=satjan|lang=got}} and Old English {{term|settan|lang=ang}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{legal|common law|historical|}} A [[feudal]] term for an [[entitlement]] to a [[freehold]] estate with a right to immediate possession; still used in technical discussions of real property law today.

